      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "As soil pH approaches 7.0, hydrolysis reactions cause dissolved iron (Fe³⁺) and manganese (Mn²⁺) to precipitate into insoluble hydroxides and oxide complexes, rendering them unavailable for root uptake. This transformation occurs because higher hydrogen ion concentrations in the root zone displace these metal cations from their soil solution, locking them into solid phases within the soil matrix. While magnesium and phosphate solubility may also be affected, the primary chemical driver for micronutrient retentions here is the shift from ionic forms to chemically stable precipitates that grapevine roots cannot extract.",
